96
00:07:25,167 --> 00:07:27,956
What should I know about docker events?

97
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:32,907
The Docker engine records an event for everything that happens:

98
00:07:32,907 --> 00:07:37,034
a container is created, started, dies (with its exit code),

99
00:07:37,034 --> 00:07:40,951
is killed by the kernel for using too much memory (oom),

100
00:07:40,951 --> 00:07:47,387
changes health status, is destroyed; images are pulled, networks connected, volumes mounted.

101
00:07:47,387 --> 00:07:51,374
docker events streams them live or replays a time window.

102
00:07:51,374 --> 00:07:58,159
When a container "keeps restarting" or "disappeared", the events tell you what happened and when.

103
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:03,587
Do a full life cycle, then replay its events.

104
00:08:03,587 --> 00:08:07,929
--until with the current time (in seconds since 1970,

105
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:14,975
date +%s) makes docker events print the window and exit, instead of streaming forever:

106
00:08:14,975 --> 00:08:17,515
Every event carries attributes:

107
00:08:17,515 --> 00:08:19,399
for die, the exit code:

108
00:08:19,933 --> 00:08:22,663
Every event carries attributes:

109
00:08:22,663 --> 00:08:24,688
for die, the exit code:

